WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.024
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:06.324 --> 00:00:09.745
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.045 --> 00:00:15.739
Unit fourteen: Photojournalism. Today you will describe images with participle phrases.

4
00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:18.598
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.998
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.072
Photo caption.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:29.430
Photo caption means the words under a photo that explain it.

8
00:00:30.017 --> 00:00:32.573
The photo caption named everyone in the picture.

9
00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:35.882
Your turn. Say: Photo caption.

10
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:39.920
Photo caption.

11
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:41.847
Photojournalist.

12
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.951
Photojournalist means a person who tells news stories with photographs.

13
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.223
The photojournalist travelled to the flood zone.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.957
Your turn. Say: Photojournalist.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.126
Photojournalist.

16
00:00:58.883 --> 00:01:00.569
Visual storytelling.

17
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:05.394
Visual storytelling means telling a story mainly through pictures.

18
00:01:05.983 --> 00:01:08.563
Visual storytelling can move readers deeply.

19
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:12.418
Your turn. Say: Visual storytelling.

20
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:16.790
Visual storytelling.

21
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:18.946
Ethical guideline.

22
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.389
Ethical guideline means a rule about what is morally right.

23
00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.877
An ethical guideline forbids staged photos.

24
00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:30.670
Your turn. Say: Ethical guideline.

25
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:34.976
Ethical guideline.

26
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:37.043
Image manipulation.

27
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.907
Image manipulation means changing a photo to give a false impression.

28
00:01:42.517 --> 00:01:44.992
Image manipulation destroys trust.

29
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:49.025
Your turn. Say: Image manipulation.

30
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:53.936
Image manipulation.

31
00:01:54.683 --> 00:01:56.010
Iconic photo.

32
00:01:57.017 --> 00:02:01.060
Iconic photo means a famous photograph that people remember for decades.

33
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:03.847
The iconic photo shaped public opinion.

34
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.393
Your turn. Say: Iconic photo.

35
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:11.838
Iconic photo.

36
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:13.770
Press pass.

37
00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:19.488
Press pass means an official card allowing a journalist to report at an event.

38
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.862
The press pass opened the gate.

39
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.079
Your turn. Say: Press pass.

40
00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:29.062
Press pass.

41
00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:31.108
Frontline coverage.

42
00:02:32.117 --> 00:02:35.869
Frontline coverage means reporting from the place where events are happening.

43
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:38.854
Frontline coverage requires great courage.

44
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.578
Your turn. Say: Frontline coverage.

45
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:46.887
Frontline coverage.

46
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.595
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:59.375
Visal, here is a photo caption: 'A girl, holding a rescued puppy, smiles after the flood.' Why two commas?

48
00:03:00.483 --> 00:03:09.846
The participle phrase 'holding a rescued puppy' adds detail about the girl. Photo captions often use -ing and -ed phrases to be short.

49
00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.764
Do captions use -ed too?

50
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:21.555
Yes. 'Rescued from the flood, the puppy was cared for by volunteers.' The -ed phrase gives a passive meaning.

51
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.324
And the photographer's duty?

52
00:03:25.417 --> 00:03:34.069
An ethical guideline says photojournalists must not use image manipulation, because a changed picture can lie.

53
00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:36.629
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:40.969
Grammar focus: Participle phrases in captions.

55
00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:42.537
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.608
A girl, holding a rescued puppy, smiles after the flood.

57
00:03:47.517 --> 00:03:51.258
Rescued from the water, the puppy was cared for by volunteers.

58
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.926
The photojournalist, working near the front line, took an iconic photo.

59
00:03:56.867 --> 00:04:04.593
Use an -ing phrase for an action happening at the same time and an -ed phrase for a passive meaning. Put commas around the phrase.

60
00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:09.419
Be careful! Don't say: A girl hold a puppy, smiles.

61
00:04:11.367 --> 00:04:14.341
Say: A girl, holding a puppy, smiles.

62
00:04:14.641 --> 00:04:16.502
Use an -ing phrase with commas.

63
00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.103
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.470
The Power of a Picture.

65
00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:26.395
A single photograph can tell a story faster than a long article.

66
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:33.382
A photojournalist, carrying a camera and a press pass, often works close to danger during frontline coverage.

67
00:04:34.367 --> 00:04:38.607
Each image needs a clear photo caption, written to explain who, where and when.

68
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.141
An ethical guideline forbids image manipulation, because a changed picture can mislead the public.

69
00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:49.456
Visual storytelling is powerful only when it is honest.

70
00:04:50.567 --> 00:04:55.044
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:55.883 --> 00:04:58.754
A girl, blank a puppy, smiles after the flood.

72
00:04:59.054 --> 00:05:02.394
A, holding. B, held to. C, holds.

73
00:05:05.933 --> 00:05:07.805
The answer is A: holding.

74
00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.263
The words under a photo are the photo blank.

75
00:05:11.563 --> 00:05:15.031
A, caption. B, pass. C, guideline.

76
00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:20.148
The answer is A: caption.

77
00:05:21.283 --> 00:05:24.560
Changing a photo to give a false impression is image blank.

78
00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.804
A, storytelling. B, manipulation. C, coverage.

79
00:05:32.367 --> 00:05:34.749
The answer is B: manipulation.

80
00:05:35.883 --> 00:05:37.477
Choose the correct caption.

81
00:05:37.777 --> 00:05:45.691
A, Rescued from water, the puppy was safe.. B, Rescue from water, the puppy safe was.. C, Rescuing water, the puppy is safe..

82
00:05:49.233 --> 00:05:52.591
The answer is A: Rescued from water, the puppy was safe..

83
00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.246
According to the reading, visual storytelling is powerful only when it is…

84
00:05:58.546 --> 00:06:02.071
A, honest. B, colourful. C, expensive.

85
00:06:05.633 --> 00:06:07.482
The answer is A: honest.

86
00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:11.969
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.817 --> 00:06:17.208
Why is it important that a news photo is never digitally altered to change its meaning?

88
00:06:26.017 --> 00:06:27.552
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:27.852 --> 00:06:50.223
A news photo is accepted as evidence of what really happened. If it is digitally altered, readers receive a false picture and may stop trusting all journalism. A changed image can also hurt innocent people. For this reason, ethical guidelines allow only small technical corrections such as brightness.

90
00:06:50.523 --> 00:06:52.174
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:53.083 --> 00:06:57.872
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle phrases in captions.

92
00:06:58.172 --> 00:07:02.148
Today's value is truthfulness. Tell the truth and check the facts.

93
00:07:02.448 --> 00:07:05.297
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:05.597 --> 00:07:08.610
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

